CAT = "pltLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("A parent asks to see her child's school records. Under FERPA, the school must:",
 "Let her inspect them", ["Refuse until the child is 18", "Show only the grades page", "Charge her a fee to view them"],
 "The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act gives parents the right to inspect and review their child's education records and to ask for corrections."),
("A teacher wants to post students' test scores with their names on the hallway bulletin board. Under FERPA, this is:",
 "A violation of privacy", ["Allowed if scores are high", "Required for accountability", "Allowed for students under 10"],
 "Test scores are part of education records. Publicly posting them with names discloses private information without consent."),
("A teacher notices bruises in the shape of a hand on a first grader and the child seems afraid to go home. What must the teacher do?",
 "Report it as state law requires", ["Wait for clear proof of abuse", "Call the parents to ask first", "Tell only a trusted colleague"],
 "Teachers are mandated reporters. They must report reasonable suspicion of abuse or neglect to the authorities named in state law; proof is not required."),
("A teacher who makes a good-faith report of suspected child abuse that turns out to be unfounded is generally:",
 "Protected from liability", ["Liable for damages", "Required to pay a fine", "Barred from teaching"],
 "State reporting laws generally grant immunity to people who report in good faith, so fear of being wrong should not stop a report."),
("In Tinker v. Des Moines (1969), the Supreme Court ruled that students' symbolic speech, such as wearing armbands in protest, is protected unless it:",
 "Substantially disrupts school", ["Expresses a strong opinion", "Is about national politics", "Is worn during class time"],
 "Tinker held that students do not shed their free speech rights at the schoolhouse gate; schools may limit speech that materially and substantially disrupts school."),
("Before a student is suspended for a few days, Goss v. Lopez (1975) requires that the student receive:",
 "Notice and a chance to respond", ["A full jury trial first", "A lawyer paid by the school", "A written apology from staff"],
 "Goss held that students facing short suspensions have due process rights: notice of the charges and an opportunity to tell their side."),
("A teacher leaves her class of second graders alone to make copies, and a student is injured. If the family sues for negligence, the main question is whether the teacher:",
 "Failed her duty to supervise", ["Planned an engaging lesson", "Used the adopted textbook", "Wrote clear lesson objectives"],
 "Negligence involves a duty of care, a breach of that duty, and an injury caused by the breach. Teachers have a duty to supervise students."),
("Each year a teacher copies an entire published workbook for every student instead of buying it. Under copyright law this is:",
 "Likely infringement", ["Always fair use in schools", "Allowed if no one profits", "Allowed after one year"],
 "Copying a whole consumable work to replace buying it harms the market for the work, so it is unlikely to be fair use."),
("In Brown v. Board of Education (1954), the Supreme Court ruled that:",
 "Segregated schools are unequal", ["Prayer in schools is required", "States must fund private schools", "Students may not wear armbands"],
 "Brown held that separate public schools for students of different races are inherently unequal, a key decision for equal access."),
("In Lau v. Nichols (1974), the Supreme Court held that schools must:",
 "Give English learners real access", ["Teach only in English", "Separate students by language", "Test all students in English"],
 "Lau held that giving English learners the same materials without language support denies them a meaningful education."),
("In Plyler v. Doe (1982), the Supreme Court held that public schools may not:",
 "Turn away undocumented children", ["Require school uniforms", "Give standardized tests", "Assign homework on weekends"],
 "Plyler held that states cannot deny children a free public education because of their immigration status."),
("A parent asks that a picture book be removed from the school library because she dislikes its ideas. Which principle is most relevant?",
 "Intellectual freedom", ["Due process for staff", "Copyright fair use", "Mandated reporting"],
 "Intellectual freedom protects students' right to access ideas. Schools use a formal review process rather than removing books on one complaint."),
("A teacher leads her class in a daily prayer. Under the First Amendment, this is:",
 "Not allowed in a public school", ["Allowed if it is short", "Allowed if parents agree to it", "Required by federal law"],
 "School-sponsored prayer violates the Establishment Clause. Students may pray privately, but a teacher may not lead prayer in class."),
("Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 mainly bars:",
 "Sex discrimination in schools", ["Copyright infringement", "Disclosure of student records", "Corporal punishment by staff"],
 "Title IX prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in education programs that receive federal funds, including athletics."),
("Tenure for a public school teacher generally means that the teacher:",
 "Can be dismissed only with due process", ["Can never be dismissed for any reason", "Earns a higher fixed salary", "Need not renew her license"],
 "Tenure gives procedural protection: dismissal requires cause and due process. The rules and timelines vary by state."),
("A teacher wants to discuss a student's learning disability with another teacher who has no role in the student's education. Under FERPA and good practice, she should:",
 "Share it only with those who need to know", ["Share it freely with all staff", "Share it with the class to help", "Post it in the staff room"],
 "Confidential information is shared only with school officials with a legitimate educational interest in the student."),
]
